915 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534915 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "986ff1d6-a817-5ff7-8aed-97a0eafbf95e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535065 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c354aefb-fffc-5ecc-8cac-e2625bd63b47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535065 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535091 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "003358a9-f2af-59ba-b62d-17d3096b53b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535091 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535102 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535102 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27637d35-ea70-516e-ae4d-2f2b9a81ce00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535102 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535111 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535111 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77786643-b29c-5c9c-ac94-7ecbfe592858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535111 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535112 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535112 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77da484d-f99e-564b-b4c3-19d24cde8a96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535112 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186308687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535126 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535126 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4ecef28-9728-5f9f-986f-392086d6b9d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535126 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186546000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1045 "Order of Numerical Methods" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186545906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545186 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4178e725-dd3e-5e50-9f29-c459bc20e2d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545349 #edb/ref 1080 "0.006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50111e51-5d29-56d4-8ecb-f3dd1e391393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545754 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545754 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545754 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186545349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545754 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "abfff1ee-ddd9-547e-a06a-6674882e92fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186545754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545832 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.20$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.20)≈0.234\\,375.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.08,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.08)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83896362-1e2d-5431-a36e-61683a703ea6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545832 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.08,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.08)≈\\frac{0.234\\,375}{39.0625}=0.006.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186546000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186545906 